\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The same geometry as Fig.~\ref {fig:rve-dimensions}, now meshed with standard displacement-based finite elements. The outer boundary (red) carries the prescribed affine macro-strain Dirichlet condition; the remaining boundary and interior nodes, including the hole boundary, are free. The cell geometry has $D_4$ (square) symmetry, but the PANN construction in this paper does not assume or exploit it -- see Section~\ref {sec:related-klein-linden}.}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rve-geometry}{{2}{6}{The same geometry as Fig.~\ref {fig:rve-dimensions}, now meshed with standard displacement-based finite elements. The outer boundary (red) carries the prescribed affine macro-strain Dirichlet condition; the remaining boundary and interior nodes, including the hole boundary, are free. The cell geometry has $D_4$ (square) symmetry, but the PANN construction in this paper does not assume or exploit it -- see Section~\ref {sec:related-klein-linden}}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The full three-dimensional macro-strain training domain $(E_{11},E_{22},\gamma _{12})$: the ten training trajectories form five tension/compression pairs, one pair per swept shear level $\gamma _{12}\in \{0,\pm 0.05,\pm 0.1\}$, so each panel here fixes $\gamma _{12}$ at its final value for that pair and shows the $(E_{11},E_{22})$ path of both members. Within each panel, the boustrophedon (zig-zag) tension branch sweeps $E_{22}\in [0,2]$ and the shorter compression branch sweeps $E_{22}\in [-0.1,0]$, both starting from the origin. FOM solves along all ten paths generate every training snapshot used to build the POD basis, the decoder $\mathcal  N$, and the ECM/MAW--ECM cubature rules of Section~\ref {sec:rom-comparison}.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:training-trajectories}{{3}{6}{The full three-dimensional macro-strain training domain $(E_{11},E_{22},\gamma _{12})$: the ten training trajectories form five tension/compression pairs, one pair per swept shear level $\gamma _{12}\in \{0,\pm 0.05,\pm 0.1\}$, so each panel here fixes $\gamma _{12}$ at its final value for that pair and shows the $(E_{11},E_{22})$ path of both members. Within each panel, the boustrophedon (zig-zag) tension branch sweeps $E_{22}\in [0,2]$ and the shorter compression branch sweeps $E_{22}\in [-0.1,0]$, both starting from the origin. FOM solves along all ten paths generate every training snapshot used to build the POD basis, the decoder $\mathcal N$, and the ECM/MAW--ECM cubature rules of Section~\ref {sec:rom-comparison}}{figure.3}{}}
