\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The full displacement field $\bm  {u}=(\bm  {u}_d,\bm  {u}_f)$ at the last (most deformed) configuration of each of the ten training trajectories, reconstructed from the converged FOM solution $\bm  {u}_f$ (free part) and the prescribed affine Dirichlet data $\bm  {u}_d$ (boundary part), plotted on the deformed configuration and colored by the nodal displacement magnitude $\|\bm  {u}\|$ on a single shared color scale across all ten panels (reference coordinates $x,y\in [-1,1]$). The five tension-branch trajectories ($E_{22}=2$: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9) show large, roughly equibiaxial bulging with the circular hole rounding toward a square, increasingly skewed by shear; the five compression-branch trajectories ($E_{22}=-0.1$: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10) show a strongly anisotropic, flattened cell with an elliptical hole. The near-$D_4$ shape visible for the unsheared equibiaxial case (trajectory~1) is a property of this particular deformation state, not an assumption used anywhere in the PANN construction -- see Section~\ref {sec:related-klein-linden}.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:displacement-field}{{4}{7}{The full displacement field $\bm u=(\bm u_d,\bm u_f)$ at the last (most deformed) configuration of each of the ten training trajectories, reconstructed from the converged FOM solution $\bm u_f$ (free part) and the prescribed affine Dirichlet data $\bm u_d$ (boundary part), plotted on the deformed configuration and colored by the nodal displacement magnitude $\|\bm u\|$ on a single shared color scale across all ten panels (reference coordinates $x,y\in [-1,1]$). The five tension-branch trajectories ($E_{22}=2$: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9) show large, roughly equibiaxial bulging with the circular hole rounding toward a square, increasingly skewed by shear; the five compression-branch trajectories ($E_{22}=-0.1$: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10) show a strongly anisotropic, flattened cell with an elliptical hole. The near-$D_4$ shape visible for the unsheared equibiaxial case (trajectory~1) is a property of this particular deformation state, not an assumption used anywhere in the PANN construction -- see Section~\ref {sec:related-klein-linden}}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Comparison against the admissibility checklist. ``Exact'' means satisfied by construction for every parameter value, not approximated by training or data augmentation. ``Group-free anisotropy'' means the model represents a general anisotropic material without committing to a named symmetry class (isotropic, transversely isotropic, cubic, orthotropic, \dots  ) or a learned member of one. ``Growth exercised'' means the model's volumetric term actually diverges/was actually tested at $J\to 0^+$, as opposed to being stated as a requirement but bypassed because the worked examples are (nearly) incompressible.}}{7}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:literature}{{1}{7}{Comparison against the admissibility checklist. ``Exact'' means satisfied by construction for every parameter value, not approximated by training or data augmentation. ``Group-free anisotropy'' means the model represents a general anisotropic material without committing to a named symmetry class (isotropic, transversely isotropic, cubic, orthotropic, \dots ) or a learned member of one. ``Growth exercised'' means the model's volumetric term actually diverges/was actually tested at $J\to 0^+$, as opposed to being stated as a requirement but bypassed because the worked examples are (nearly) incompressible}{table.1}{}}
