\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Held-out test-trajectory relative $L^2$ error, all six reported quantities of Table~\ref {tab:master}, for all six model variants: the tier-1 pure-regression ANN, the tier-2 free hyperelastic ANN, the two certified polyconvex cores, and both HPROM--ANN operating modes. The uncertified ANNs (tiers 1--2) are competitive with, and on several components better than, the certified models on every accuracy metric shown here -- consistent with Sections~\ref {sec:c1-regression} and~\ref {sec:non-polyconvex}'s point that accuracy alone cannot distinguish a certified model from an uncertified one; only the cyclic-work and rank-one curvature audits can.}}{29}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:full-comparison-bars}{{12}{29}{Held-out test-trajectory relative $L^2$ error, all six reported quantities of Table~\ref {tab:master}, for all six model variants: the tier-1 pure-regression ANN, the tier-2 free hyperelastic ANN, the two certified polyconvex cores, and both HPROM--ANN operating modes. The uncertified ANNs (tiers 1--2) are competitive with, and on several components better than, the certified models on every accuracy metric shown here -- consistent with Sections~\ref {sec:c1-regression} and~\ref {sec:non-polyconvex}'s point that accuracy alone cannot distinguish a certified model from an uncertified one; only the cyclic-work and rank-one curvature audits can}{figure.12}{}}
